# Exceptions for scripts/public_check.py (rule: AGENTS.md "Public safety").
# The list is read from the source the files are read from: the commit of --rev, whatever is
# checked out; the index with --staged; else the working tree.
# One entry per line, each with its reason after '#':
#   <path glob>   # reason     covers every hit in the matching files
#   <path>:<line> # reason     covers the hits on that line only; it stops covering them when a
#                              line is inserted above, and the hit it no longer covers names it
#   <path> <label> sha256:<digest>  # reason
#                              covers every content hit of that public label in that one file
#                              whose text has that digest, on any line, so it keeps covering its
#                              hit when lines move; never a file-name hit, a message or an identity
#   commit:<full sha> author|committer  # reason
#                              covers the identity hits of that role on that commit only, for
#                              history already published; the same identity elsewhere still fails
#   commit:<full sha> message  # reason
#                              covers the message hits (tracker references included) of that
#                              commit only, for history already published; the same text in any
#                              other commit still fails
# The digest is the first 16 lowercase hex digits of SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the hit's
# whole match; --digests prints it for every public-label hit, and nothing else prints one. The
# path is a repository path, spaces allowed; the label and the digest are the last two words.
# What a committed entry reveals: its path, its label and, for a digest entry, a digest of text
# the file already shows. No committed entry keys a local term (a local-term hit of
# .public-check.local): a digest of one is refused, as a word list would reverse it, and a glob or
# path:line entry that covers only local-term hits is warned about, as it tells readers where a
# private term sits. The local list allows its own term by the term's line <n>, in a line of its
# own that is never committed: 'allow:<path> local-term:<n>  # reason', which covers that term's
# content hits in that file, on any line.
# File entries never cover commit messages. An unused entry, one that matches no hit:
# - a glob or path:line entry fails a check that has the local list, naming its line and form, so
#   stale exceptions cannot hide a later leak; without the list it is only warned about as
#   unverifiable, as it may cover a local-term hit the run cannot see;
# - a digest entry fails with or without the local list, as it covers no local term; where that
#   list switches its label off it is warned about as unverifiable, as its hits are not looked for;
# - a local list's 'allow:' entry is only warned about, as one clone's list serves every branch it
#   checks;
# - a commit entry counts only in the gate of a range that holds its commit, which alone sees its
#   hits.
